
  > A very common use case — you have a messy list of emails (maybe from a sign-up form) and you want to clean it up.
    > Why HashSet here? One line (new HashSet<>(rawEmails)) does the dedup work that would otherwise take a nested loop or a stream().distinct().

# Removing Duplicates from a List of Emails

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class EmailDeduplicator
{
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Pretend this came from a college fest registration form
        List<String> rawEmails = new ArrayList<>();
        rawEmails.add("arjun.sharma@example.com");
        rawEmails.add("priya.patel@example.com");
        rawEmails.add("arjun.sharma@example.com");   // duplicate
        rawEmails.add("rahul.verma@example.com");
        rawEmails.add("priya.patel@example.com");    // duplicate
        rawEmails.add("sneha.iyer@example.com");

        // The trick: pass the list straight into a HashSet constructor.
        // HashSet will silently drop the duplicates for us.
        Set<String> uniqueEmails = new HashSet<>(rawEmails);

        System.out.println("Raw list size:     " + rawEmails.size());
        System.out.println("Unique list size:  " + uniqueEmails.size());
        System.out.println("Unique emails: " + uniqueEmails);
    }
}

OutPut :
Raw list size:     6
Unique list size:  4
Unique emails: [rahul.verma@example.com, sneha.iyer@example.com, arjun.sharma@example.com, priya.patel@example.com]
